How to use the two licences
---------------------------

The licences are alternatives, not conditions stacked on top of each other.
Pick the one that fits what you are doing; you do not have to satisfy both at
once.

  * MPL-2.0 is weak, file-level copyleft. Change a Kerosene file and
    distribute it, and that file must stay MPL-2.0; files you write yourself
    -- your game code, levels, scripts, assets -- carry whatever terms you
    like. There is no linking stage, so a closed-source game statically linked
    to the engine owes nothing beyond this notice and a pointer to the source.

  * LGPL-3.0-or-later is stronger copyleft on the engine as a whole. If you
    modify Kerosene and distribute your version, the modified engine must be
    released under the LGPL. Code you write *against* the engine is not the
    engine: the LGPL lets you keep it closed, provided the engine part can
    still be replaced. Rust links statically, so that means shipping the
    engine as a replaceable library or shipping your object files for
    relinking -- real but ordinary work. The MPL arm exists precisely so that
    anyone who finds that too much ceremony can take the other one.

Why offer both? So the choice is yours, not the project's. Take MPL-2.0 for
the simplest possible shipping story; take the LGPL if you want the stronger
guarantee that a modified engine stays open.

Whichever you take, the project's preference is the same: if you change
Kerosene itself, please contribute the change back as a pull request instead
of releasing a modified Kerosene of your own. Use the updated engine, and the
fix exists once, reviewed and maintained, rather than once per fork. Releasing
a fork is permitted under either licence; a pull request is simply better for
everyone who builds on Kerosene.

Independent reimplementation
----------------------------

Kerosene is an independent reimplementation. It is not affiliated with,
endorsed by, or sponsored by Valve Corporation or id Software LLC, and it
contains no source code, assets, or data files from the Source engine, the
Source SDK, Hammer, Quake, or any other product of either company.

The project was called VoidEngine until August 2026. It was renamed because
an unrelated engine built on id Tech 6 already uses that name; there is no
relationship between the two, and no code, assets or formats are shared with
it. Kerosene is likewise unaffiliated with Kerosene Games, a game publisher
of that name.

It is not a *clean-room* reimplementation, and does not claim to be: it
was written with knowledge of how id's published compilers work, so the
provenance section below states plainly which modules follow that
structure. Everything here was written from scratch in Rust.

"Valve", "Source", "Source 2", "Hammer", "Half-Life", "Portal", "Steam"
and "Quake" are trademarks of their respective owners. Where those names
appear in Kerosene's documentation and source comments, they are used
nominatively -- to say what a piece of this project is analogous to, or
which published technique it follows -- and no claim of affiliation is
made or implied.

Kerosene defines its own file formats. They are deliberately named and
byte-tagged so they cannot be confused with anyone else's:

    .keromap  .kerobsp  .keroprt  .keroleak
    .kerotex  .keromat  .keromdl  .vault

None of them is compatible with, readable by, or produced from any Valve
or id format. Kerosene cannot open Source or Quake content and does not
attempt to.

